A flute can cross several limits in a single breath. It can go from an airy note to a clear pitch, jump an octave, then break into a rough sound. The same air stream can color a note, sustain it, or push the flute into a different mode of vibration. So the question is bigger than “How much noise can it take?” It’s “What kinds of noise can this structure turn into something, and where does each kind take it?”

Try holding one note on a flute and changing one thing at a time. Blow harder and listen for the register jump. Keep the breath steady and roll the flute slightly to hear how much air can remain audible before the pitch gets hard to hear. Then play the same note with a deliberate breathy attack and ask someone to copy it. You’ve just tested three different limits: what the flute can sustain, what a listener can recognize, and what a player can reproduce. A sound might be physically possible and musically striking while still being almost impossible to summon on cue.

The same experiment works across domains if you pick what you’re varying and what you’re trying to preserve.

Domain	What you vary	What you listen for
Bridge	Load amplitude and frequency	When ordinary motion becomes a large response. A small repeating force near a natural frequency can matter more than a larger isolated push.
Language	Missing words, reordered phrases, substitutions	When the reader stops filling in gaps and starts inferring a different meaning. Losing the and losing never are both one-word deletions.
Story	Scene order, missing events, unreliable narration	When ambiguity stops enriching the story and breaks the audience’s ability to follow cause and effect. Or when the same ambiguity makes a second coherent story possible.
Mortgage workflow	Missing information, contradictory information, handoff delays	When a file can still advance, when it needs verification, and when the decision changes. A missing pay stub and a contradictory income figure can interrupt the same step for very different reasons.
Pantheon	Compress an idea, change its medium, or omit its original prompt	What survives the translation, what gets invented in the gaps, and which inventions can become useful ideas in their own right.

There’s an extra dimension worth testing: does the system return to where it started when you remove the disturbance? Play the flute softly again and the original note may return. Tell someone the missing sentence in a story and they may never be able to read the opening the old way. The input has returned to baseline, but the reader has changed. That gives you a way to tell a temporary effect from a change the system carries forward.

For the Pantheon, I’d run the first experiment on “It paints the area, then it becomes the area.” Give one reader the full line, another “It paints the area,” and a third a silent scene of someone repainting a trail marker until hikers start treating the new route as the official trail. Ask each what changed and what caused it. Can they recover the passage from a mark on the world to a mark people use to decide how the world works? If the silent scene carries that relationship, you’ve found something that survives a change of medium. If one reader sees vandalism and another sees institution-building, you’ve found the part the original phrase leaves open. That’s the start of a cross-domain atlas of what a structure absorbs, what it amplifies, and what makes it become something else.